SP  - 1279
EP  - 1289
DO  - 10.1097/00007890-199605150-00001
AN  - 8629285
AB  - Symptomatic cytomegalovirus (CMV*) infection occurs in 8%, 29%, 25%, and 39% of kidney, liver, heart, and heartlung transplant recipients, respectively (1). CMV is transmitted to transplant recipients mainly by the donor organ and to a lesser degree by blood products (2). In the solid organ transplant recipient, CMV is related to: (1) infectious diseases syndromes and organ involvement (lung, liver, gastro-intestinal, retinitis, among others); (2) increased immunosuppression (hence its frequent association with other opportunistic infections, e.g., fungal and Pneumocystis) (3); and (3) although controversial, acute and chronic allograft injury (4, 5). The diagnosis of CMV infection is based on the recognition of cytomegalic inclusion bodies in tissue. In addition, tube cell and shell vial culture techniques are used to detect replicating CMV in body fluids and in tissue. Demonstration of CMV in blood (viremia) or in tissue biopsies is highly correlated with the presence of clinically active or impending infection, whereas isolation of the virus from urine or respiratory secretions is frequently a marker of asymptomatic infection. Direct detection of antigenemia in blood leukocytes of transplant recipients is sensitive and more rapid than the shell vial technique, and provides an early marker of active CMV infection (6, 7). Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) techniques can detect CMV DNA in peripheral blood leukocytes (8), serum or plasma (9), and other clinical specimens, as well as CMV RNA in peripheral blood leukocytes. The clinical usefulness of PCR is currently being evaluated. Risk factor analyses have provided important clues to identify those patients at high risk for symptomatic CMV infection, reflecting in part the pathogenesis of CMV following solid organ transplantation. A lack of cellular and humoral immunity, as defined by the absence of anti-CMV antibodies, places the recipient in the high-risk category upon receiving a CMV seropositive donor's organ or other exogenous source of CMV (e.g., blood transfusion). In addition, antilymphocyte immunoglobulins (antithymocyte globulin, antilymphocyte globulin, OKT3 mAb), used either as induction therapy or for allograft rejection treatment, enhance the rate of symptomatic CMV infection, especially in CMV seropositive individuals (10, 11). Although more information is needed about the biology of CMV, viral load may play an important role in identifying patients at higher risk. For example, the high rate of symptomatic CMV infection in lung and gastrointestinal transplant recipients may reflect the transplantation of a higher latent CMV load contained in a larger amount of tissue with increased endothelial and lymphoid compartments. Thus, prophylactic measures found to be effective for renal transplantation may not apply to lung or gastrointestinal transplantation. Likewise, lessons learned from CMV infection in bone marrow transplantation can be only partially extrapolated to the nonhomogenous field of solid organ transplantation due to differences in pathogenesis. The ideal CMV prophylactic regimen should be characterized as: (1) effective in an oral formulation if frequent administration is required or in an intravenous formulation that can be given at infrequent intervals (i.e., weekly); (2) safe, thus requiring minimal laboratory evaluations and having a wide therapeutic range to avoid monitoring of levels; (3) having minimal interactions with conventional “transplantation” medications; (4) pan virustatic-cidal to cover not only CMV but other herpes family viruses (herpes simplex virus, varicella-zoster virus, Epstein-Barr virus, human herpesvirus 6), with a low chance of inducing antiviral resistance; (5) administered only to patients at risk for symptomatic CMV infection; and (6) cost effective. Based on the current state-of-the-art CMV prophylaxis and treatment, significant advances need to be made in order to achieve the above goals. Current prophylactic approaches vary widely among different transplant programs. Reasons for the discrepancies reflect the absence of large, multicentered, randomized trials. In addition, results of small single-center studies are frequently difficult to reproduce, as differences in endpoints, definitions, viral surveillance (frequency, methodology), type of immunosuppressive regimen, patient populations, and frequency of antilymphocyte antibody treatment are common. More importantly, inherent differences among the type of solid organ transplanted need to be considered. This results in a myriad of prophylactic regimens, many of which may be insufficient or even excessive, from both effectiveness and cost perspectives. This Overview summarizes currently available data addressing the prophylaxis of CMV, clarifies key points that should serve for the design of rational prophylaxis regimens utilizing current antiviral agents, and outlines future alternative strategies as new agents and approaches become available. SERONEGATIVE CMV BLOOD PRODUCTS AND PROTECTIVE MATCHING The use of CMV seronegative blood products was first shown to be effective in preventing CMV infection in CMV seronegative bone marrow transplant recipients, and subsequently in studies in solid organ transplant recipients (12). A disadvantage of CMV seronegative blood products is their scarcity in areas with a high prevalence of CMV antibodies. In this situation, filtered or leukocyte-poor blood products, which are associated with a very low risk of CMV transmission, are useful (12). CMV seronegative, filtered blood products or leukocyte-poor blood should be used for all transfusions given to seronegative recipients (12). Knowledge of CMV serostatus of the donor and recipient before transplantation will predict which patients will develop CMV disease. Although the patient at highest risk for CMV infection is the seronegative recipient of a seropositive allograft, protective matching of seronegative donors and seronegative recipients is not practical due to the scarcity of donor organs. VACCINATION In theory, one of the simplest interventions to prevent CMV infection and disease after solid organ transplantation would be immunization of seronegative recipients with a vaccine given once in anticipation of future viral challenge. A candidate live attenuated CMV Towne strain vaccine was first tested in the mid-1970s in normal volunteers, and randomized, placebo-controlled, double-blind trials of the Towne vaccine in renal transplant recipients subsequently demonstrated that the vaccine was both safe and immunogenic. In two separate studies, the vaccine did not decrease the incidence of CMV infection or disease but decreased the severity of symptoms in the “high-risk” (D+/R-) group (13, 14). Renewed efforts are underway to evaluate subunit vaccines containing recombinant glycoproteins (B, H) by several biotechnology companies (15). Although complete protection against CMV may not be achieved by a vaccine, partial protection against the serious manifestations of CMV infection and disease could be possible. Moreover, a suboptimal vaccine could be considered in “combination” with other current regimens, such as antiviral agents and/or immunoglobulin. PASSIVE IMMUNOPROPHYLAXIS Since immunity to CMV prior to transplantation decreases the incidence of CMV disease following transplantation, boosting the immune system after transplantation should have a beneficial effect in decreasing CMV disease. Expanding the number and function of virus-specific cytotoxic T lymphocytes to reduce the incidence of CMV disease following bone marrow transplantation is now a reality (16). In addition, the humoral CMV immune response can theoretically be boosted with specific CMV immunoglobulin (Ig). Passive immunization with intravenous Ig has been used in an attempt to prevent CMV infection and disease in solid organ transplant recipients. Both unscreened (unselected) and hyperimmune globulin preparations have been studied. CMV hyperimmune globulin (CMVIG) is obtained from CMV seropositive donors and standardized for a high titer of CMV antibody content. Thus it is 4- to 8-fold enriched in anti-CMV titer as compared with unselected immune globulin. The currently approved unselected polyclonal Ig preparations are difficult to compare because of differences in the process of manufacturing, donor pool sources, and IgG compositions even among lots of the same preparation. Furthermore, there are no standardized laboratory assays for evaluation of their efficacy. Neutralization assays have been proposed to predict activity of the different preparations, but a direct correlation between CMV antibodies measured by neutralization, ELISA, or other assays and protection in humans has not been demonstrated. Indeed, in the murine CMV model, monoclonal antibodies that are protective against lethal murine CMV challenge are not neutralizing and vice versa (17). The incidence of adverse effects related to Ig administration is less than 5% and manifestations are generally mild and self-limited. Anaphylactic reactions have been described in patients with IgA deficiency given preparations that contain IgA. Recent reports have demonstrated contamination of some Ig preparations from specific manufacturers with hepatitis C virus (18). However, all currently manufactured preparations require both screening of the plasma pools for anti-HCV antibody and removal of such pools from the Ig, as well as a specific additional viral inactivation step. Notably, no adventitious viral agent has ever been transmitted from an immune globulin manufactured in the United States using the traditional Conn fractionation process. Whether there is the potential for transmission of other as yet unidentified pathogens is unknown. Results from randomized controlled trials in renal transplant recipients indicate that Ig prevent CMV disease (both unscreened and hyperimmune globulin) in some (19-21) but not all trials (22)(Table 1). Based on these studies, CMVIG is now licensed by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration for use in renal transplantation. In liver transplantation, unselected Ig does not reduce CMV disease (23), while use of CMV hyperimmune globulin prevents CMV disease in non-D+/R- liver transplant recipients (24). A subsequent analysis of the latter study, however, and an additional study demonstrated a reduction in CMV disease in D+/R- liver transplant recipients given CMV hyperimmune globulin (25, 26). A randomized trial in heart, lung, and kidney transplant recipients showed no differences when acyclovir was used with or without unselected Ig (27). A secondary beneficial effect of CMVIG is that it reduces other opportunistic infection following transplantation (24). The discrepancies between the above studies likely arise because of a lack of uniformity in study design. Nevertheless, a recent meta-analysis of the use of Ig to prevent symptomatic CMV disease in transplant recipients showed a beneficial effect (common odds ratio, 0.59 [95% confidence interval, 0.39-0.86]) of Ig as compared with no treatment (28). While in this analysis no apparent advantage was provided by the use of hyperimmune globulin over unselected Ig, bone marrow and solid organ transplant recipients were pooled together; thus two separate pathogenic processes were combined. When the solid organ transplantation data are analyzed in isolation, there is evidence that hyperimmune globulin is superior to unselected Ig in preventing CMV disease (29). Together, results from randomized trials in solid organ transplants indicate that: (1) Ig confers some degree of efficacy in preventing CMV disease, being more consistent with CMVIG preparations. (2) These benefits are attenuated when antilymphocyte antibody therapy is utilized. (3) Renal transplant recipients are more likely to have a beneficial effect from CMVIG than are extrarenal (e.g., liver) transplant recipients, especially within the high-risk group (D+/R-). (4) Advantages include the relatively infrequent administration (weekly intervals) and the lack of need for continuous intravenous access. (5) Disadvantages include cost (Table 2), although current studies are underway to determine the decreased expense incurred in patient management by successfully prophylaxing CMV. Formal comparisons between effective antiviral agents and CMVIG will need to be performed to address cost and side effects (30). Presently, CMVIG alone appears to be effective in reducing CMV disease in renal recipients; this approach is cost effective, at least in D+/R- renal transplant recipients (31). In liver and perhaps in heart and pancreas transplant recipients, the high-risk (D+/R-) group requires enhanced prophylaxis, which may be achieved by combining CMVIG with antiviral agents. ANTIVIRAL THERAPY Acyclovir Acyclovir, in its phosphorylated form, acts as a competitive inhibitor of viral DNA polymerase. It should be an ideal prophylactic agent for CMV since it can be given orally and is relatively inexpensive and safe. However, it possesses little in vitro activity against CMV at clinically achievable levels. In spite of this, acyclovir was first shown to prevent CMV infection and disease in bone marrow transplant recipients (34). This led to study of the role of acyclovir in CMV prophylaxis following solid organ transplantation (Table 3). One prospective, randomized, placebo-controlled study showed that acyclovir prevented CMV infection and disease following renal transplantation (35), and a randomized but non-place-bo-controlled study showed similar results when intravenous followed by oral acyclovir was used in liver transplantation (36). However, these results have not been confirmed by other studies (37), and further analysis from the same institution that demonstrated the prophylactic efficacy of acyclovir in renal transplant recipients (including some of the patients in the original study) (35) showed, as part of a study of rejection prophylaxis regimens, no difference in the incidence of CMV disease in D+/R- patients (a subgroup of patients who were particularly benefited in the original study) (38). Side effects of acyclovir include phlebitis after intravenous infusion, renal toxicity, confusion, delirium, lethargy, light-headedness, tremors, seizures, nausea, vomiting, and rash. Although acyclovir meets many of the criteria of an ideal prophylactic drug, its efficacy as an anti-CMV compound remains to be clearly established. Based on the current data, oral acyclovir may be indicated as a primary agent for non-high-risk renal transplant recipients. If it is shown that prolonged intravenous administration is effective, such results should then be compared with intravenous ganciclovir. Evaluation of a valine ester of acyclovir (valacyclovir), with higher oral absorption and higher serum levels than oral acyclovir, has been completed in bone marrow and renal transplant patients in a multicenter, blinded fashion. Results from these studies should settle the role of acyclovir as a useful anti-CMV agent in renal transplantation. Ganciclovir Ganciclovir (9-[1,3-dihydroxy-2-propoxymethyl]guaninine) has excellent in vitro activity against all members of the herpes family of viruses. It is phosphorylated to ganciclovir 5′-monophosphate by a protein encoded by the UL97 open reading frame of human CMV, and then to the di- and triphosphate form by host cellular kinases (39). The active drug inhibits DNA polymerase and competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate to act as a terminator of biosynthesis of the viral strand. As with acyclovir, most studies of ganciclovir prophylaxis are difficult to interpret because they lack control groups or instead compare ganciclovir with acyclovir or different preparations of immune globulins (Table 3). In general, the idea of intravenous antiviral prophylaxis is to administer medication the following transplantation when patients are and intravenous is In heart transplant rec