TY  - JOUR
TI  - Valaciclovir Reduces the Incidence of Cytomegalovirus Disease and Acute Graft Rejection in CMV-Seronegative Recipients of a Seropositive Cadaveric Renal Allograft
AU  - Lowance Dc
AU  - C Legendre
AU  - H-H Neumayer
AU  - Douglas J. Norman
AU  - G Coggon
AU  - I Lee
AU  - Adam Crisp
JO  - Transplantation
PY  - 1998
VL  - 65
IS  - Supplement
SP  - 94
EP  - 94
DO  - 10.1097/00007890-199805131-00061
AB  - 61 Objective: To evaluate the safety and efficacy of valaciclovir(VALTREX™/ZELITREX™) for prophylaxis of CMV disease/syndrome in high-risk donor-positive recipient-negative (D+R-) patients. Study Design: Placebo (PBO)-controlled, international, double-blind, randomised trial in 208 renal allograft recipients. Methods: Patients received valaciclovir (2g 4x daily; adjusted per creatinine clearance) or PBO for 90 days and were evaluated for 6 months post-transplant. The primary end-point was the development of protocol-defined, laboratory-confirmed, clinical CMV disease. Analysis was by Cox's regression on the intent-to-treat population.Results: Fifty-five confirmed CMV disease episodes, (41 episodes in 39 placebo patients; 14 episodes in 14 valaciclovir patients) were verified by an End-points Committee. Only 3 cases occurred during valaciclovir treatment versus 41 cases while receiving PBO. During the 6-month study period, the incidences were 39% (PBO) and 14% (valaciclovir), with 80% of disease being prevented or delayed in valaciclovir recipients (hazard ratio=0.201; 95% CI=0.106, 0.381; p<0.0001). Organ-invasive CMV disease incidence was also significantly decreased (26% PBO, 4% valaciclovir; p<0.0001). The incidence of biopsy-proven acute rejection was reduced from 49% (PBO) to 25%(valaciclovir) during the 6-month study period (hazard ratio=0.438; 95% CI=0.264, 0.728; p=0.0014). Safety: Serum creatinine concentration at baseline and at months 1, 2 and 3 was similar between the groups. No differences in graft function and survival were noted between the two groups at baseline and study end. The nature and frequency of adverse experiences, including nephrotoxicity, were similar between the two groups, with the exception of hallucination (7% valaciclovir vs 4% PBO) and confusion (6% valaciclovir vs 2% PBO). These events were generally mild, temporary and reversible following discontinuation of medication. Conclusion: Valaciclovir is well-tolerated and effective for the prophylaxis of CMV disease in D+R- patients. The significant effect on reduction of acute rejection may portend improved long-term allograft survival. This study was sponsored by Glaxo Wellcome.
